ng that a try  i think we tried to pivot them to b2b  and figure out were there use cases for  the robot say in a bank as a greeter  and the problem was there it was not the  need wasn't there it wasn't a pain point  that were we were really solving now  interesting  ntt we did a fire sale  no one bought it we went to auction with  the assets no one bought the assets  um so they laid for about a year  uh in state so to speak and ntt who had  been an investor in jibo in the early  days did come back in and acquire those  assets and they are in fact looking for  uh  like pennies on the dime um are looking  for ways that they can still use gebo  now the problem with hardware is now the  hardware 2017 to now you know it's old  hardware so the technology has evolved  and changed the last question in terms  of early warning signs yeah and it in  particular impacted me  you heard in that little uh video  vignette him say you know uh originally  you know you're supposed to let us all  develop these applications and skills  and maybe if you had continued to do  that gebo would still be here today that  was in fact why i was brought in  originally into the company was to build  out the whole developer facing effort to  be able to recruit developers to build  skills that would then potentially be in  a marketplace or that we would buy the  skills give them for free on the robot  but the whole strategy from day one was  that you would have the developers  building and then you would have the  consumers buying which in fact is what  amazon did  uh with alexa it is what by the way the  iphone did you know for the apps they  didn't build all the apps themselves  what we ran into and this was the red  flag we shut that down because of ip  concerns because the original toolkit  that engineering built exposed internal  robot ip  and the concern was that someone could  replicate gebo too quickly if they had  access to that ip so basically we limped  along and said okay we won't do the